<article class="ltx_document">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection for
<br class="ltx_break">LLM Reinforcement Learning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ningyuan Yang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute for Interdisciplinary Information Sciences (IIIS), Tsinghua University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Weihua Du
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Language Technologies Institute (LTI), Carnegie Mellon University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Correspondence to: </span><a href="mailto:weihuad@cs.cmu.edu">weihuad@cs.cmu.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Weiwei Sun
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Language Technologies Institute (LTI), Carnegie Mellon University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sean Welleck
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Language Technologies Institute (LTI), Carnegie Mellon University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Correspondence to: </span><a href="mailto:wellecks@cmu.edu">wellecks@cmu.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yiming Yang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Language Technologies Institute (LTI), Carnegie Mellon University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Correspondence to: </span><a href="mailto:yiming@cs.cmu.edu">yiming@cs.cmu.edu</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a central post-training paradigm for large language models (LLMs), but its performance is highly sensitive to the quality of training problems. This sensitivity stems from the non-stationarity of RL: rollouts are generated by an evolving policy, and learning is shaped by exploration and reward feedback, unlike supervised fine-tuning (SFT) with fixed trajectories. As a result, prior work often relies on manual curation or simple heuristic filters (e.g., accuracy), which can admit incorrect or low-utility problems.
We propose <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GradAlign</span>, a gradient-aligned data selection method for LLM reinforcement learning that uses a small, trusted validation set to prioritize training problems whose policy gradients align with validation gradients, yielding an adaptive curriculum.
We evaluate GradAlign across three challenging data regimes: unreliable reward signals, distribution imbalance, and low-utility training corpus, showing that GradAlign consistently outperforms existing baselines, underscoring the importance of directional gradient signals in navigating non-stationary policy optimization and yielding more stable training and improved final performance. We release our implementation at <a href="https://github.com/StigLidu/GradAlign" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/StigLidu/GradAlign</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Keywords: </h6>Machine Learning, ICML
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning (RL) has become an important tool for enhancing the capabilities of large language models (LLMs) in complex tasks and environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. However, the current success of LLM RL is critically dependent on high-quality human-labeled training corpora <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which are expensive to obtain. To support large-scale RL training, researchers increasingly turn to massive, automatically collected corpora <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. These collections inevitably mix high-quality instances with trivial or misleading ones and noisy labels. This raises two key challenges: (1) how to identify instances that are suitable for a given downstream task, and (2) how to design mechanisms that prevent low-quality or misleading data from corrupting the RL training signal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing RL data selection strategies for LLM training are relatively simple and primarily rely on rule-based heuristics. For example, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al. (2025)</a></cite> retains problems with intermediate accuracy to alleviate gradient vanishing, whereas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2025c)</a></cite> prioritizes samples with accuracy near 50%, analogous to selecting samples near the decision boundary in active learning. Although such partially solved problems can be informative, these methods rely on scalar difficulty signals and do not consider whether the resulting updates move the policy toward improved downstream performance.
Other approaches score training problems using gradient-based criteria, often emphasizing large parameter updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, internal consistency within the training distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, or gradient diversity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. However, these strategies do not explicitly verify alignment with downstream optimization objectives and are typically static, failing to adapt the selection criterion as the policy evolves during RL training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GradAlign</span>, an automated gradient-informed algorithm to select training problems for LLM reinforcement learning. GradAlign leverages a small, trusted validation set and uses the policy gradient as a first-order surrogate to approximate the expected improvement in validation performance induced by policy updates. For each candidate training problem, we compute an alignment score defined as the cosine similarity between its policy gradient and the aggregated validation gradient. Training problems are then ranked by this alignment score, favoring updates that are most consistent with improving validation performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address RL non-stationarity, GradAlign adopts an adaptive curriculum that periodically recomputes validation gradients and re-scores candidate problems under the current policy, using the validation set as a directional probe rather than a direct scoring or retrieval signal.
GradAlign is particularly effective in challenging data regimes common in large-scale LLM RL, including unreliable reward signals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Skalse et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, distribution mismatch between training and downstream problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kirk et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and datasets with low-utility training instances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dodge et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. In these settings, scalar accuracy-based heuristics often fail to distinguish useful learning signals from misleading ones, whereas GradAlign consistently prioritizes updates that improve downstream performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions include the following.</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GradAlign</span>, an online RL data-selection method that selects training samples whose updates are aligned with downstream performance improvement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We identify three challenging data regimes for RL data selection and conduct targeted experiments showing that GradAlign consistently outperforms prior baselines in these settings.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Data Selection for LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Data selection has emerged as a critical lever for enhancing the efficiency and performance of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koh and Liang, 2017</a>)</cite>.
Early approaches largely relied on static heuristics to filter low-quality web text, utilizing metrics such as perplexity thresholds, language identification, deduplication, and rule-based filters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Penedo et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abbas et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Beyond filtering, optimizing the mixture of data sources has been standard practice to balance general capabilities with specific domain knowledge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xie et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
More recent strategies employ diversity-based sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or proxy models to score data quality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wettig et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
However, these methods predominantly rely on human intuition or general-purpose heuristics rather than a principled evaluation of how specific data points contribute to the model’s learning trajectory, often leading to suboptimal alignment with downstream tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Influence Estimation and Gradient Alignment</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To transcend heuristic selection, researchers have adopted Influence Functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koh et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grosse et al., 2023</a>)</cite> to theoretically quantify the impact of a training instance on validation loss.
In the context of LLMs, gradient-based methods have been successfully adapted for data selection tasks during pre-training and supervised fine-tuning (SFT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Engstrom et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
Specifically, gradient alignment techniques—which select training samples whose gradient updates maximize the cosine similarity with a trusted validation set—have proven effective for instruction tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.
Despite these advances, the application of gradient-based selection is largely confined to imitation learning paradigms (SFT).
The efficacy of such influence estimation in RL, where the model learns from self-generated rollouts and sparse rewards rather than ground-truth targets, remains significantly understudied.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>LLM Reinforcement Learning</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement Learning (RL) is increasingly central to unlocking advanced LLM capabilities, particularly for complex reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and autonomous agentic tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
While standard Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite> remains a cornerstone, recent advances such as Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and the reasoning-focused DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI et al., 2025</a>)</cite> demonstrate that RL can substantially outperform supervised baselines.
However, RL algorithms are notoriously sensitive to data quality and reward noise; for example, training on trivial or unsolvable problems can induce instability or reward hacking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lightman et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
As a result, although considerable effort has been devoted to selecting valuable data using heuristic signals such as gradients or parameter distributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the curriculum of <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">which</span> problems to present to the agent, and <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">when</span>, is typically treated as static. This overlooks the potential of dynamic, quality-aware data selection to stabilize and accelerate RL training.
In this work, we propose gradient alignment as a principled approach for dynamically selecting high-quality data during the RL process.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21492v1/teaser_v2.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/170;" width="452" height="170"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S2.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of GradAlign.<span id="S2.F1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> GradAlign uses a small validation set to estimate a coherent target gradient direction and scores large-scale training candidates by gradient alignment, selecting the top-ranked fraction to form an adaptive RL online-learning curriculum.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem Formulation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We study an online reinforcement learning (RL) setting in which, at each <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">data selection round</em>, the learner is presented with a large pool of candidate training problems that may be noisy, unlabeled, or even incorrect. The learner selects a subset of these problems for training, aiming to filter out low-quality candidates and maximize performance on a downstream evaluation task.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, at round <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, given the current policy <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, we observe <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> candidate problems <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}=\{p_{r,1},p_{r,2},\ldots,p_{r,M}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}=\{p_{r,1},p_{r,2},\ldots,p_{r,M}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> can be large (e.g., <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=5120" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5120</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=5120</annotation></semantics></math>). The data selector outputs a subset <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{r}\subseteq\mathcal{P}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{r}\subseteq\mathcal{P}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> of size <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}_{r}|=M/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}_{r}|=M/q</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> controls the selection ratio. The selected set <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is then used to train the policy <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> through multiple RL optimization steps. Each problem is associated with a reference answer and a judge model
computes a binary reward indicating whether the generated output matches the reference answer.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Because the candidate pool may be highly noisy, naively training on all candidates can be inefficient and yield limited gains. We therefore assume that each pool contains a (typically small) subset of high-quality problems that are correct and informative for the downstream task, and that selecting such problems can lead to substantial improvements. We evaluate performance on a downstream benchmark split into a validation set <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and a held-out test set <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a limited number of representative or i.i.d. examples of the downstream task. During training, data selection algorithms can access <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> to guide data selection, while <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is used only for the final evaluation. Having a validation set is common in real-world tasks, such as using few-shot examples in in-context learning or public test cases in code generation tasks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>GRPO as the Reinforcement Learning Objective</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>
is a widely adopted reinforcement learning objective
for LLM post-training. In this work, we use GRPO as a unified
training objective across all experiments to isolate the effect
of data selection from differences in RL algorithms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Given an input problem <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, the policy <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> generates
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> responses <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{y_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{y_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, each evaluated by a reward
function producing reward <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. GRPO first constructs a normalized
advantage <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> by comparing rewards within the sampled group:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{j}=\frac{r_{j}-\bar{r}}{\sqrt{\frac{1}{k}\sum_{j}(r_{j}-\bar{r})^{2}}+\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{j}=\frac{r_{j}-\bar{r}}{\sqrt{\frac{1}{k}\sum_{j}(r_{j}-\bar{r})^{2}}+\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{r}=\frac{1}{k}\sum_{j=1}^{k}r_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{r}=\frac{1}{k}\sum_{j=1}^{k}r_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
GRPO optimizes a clipped policy objective with KL
regularization:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\min\!\left(\rho_{j}(\theta)\hat{A}_{j},\,\text{clip}(\rho_{j}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)\hat{A}_{j}\right)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.653em;" width="1.653em"><mi>min</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>clip</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}\!\left[\min\!\left(\rho_{j}(\theta)\hat{A}_{j},\,\text{clip}(\rho_{j}(\theta),1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon)\hat{A}_{j}\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle-\beta_{\mathrm{KL}}\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\left[\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>ref</mi></msub><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\beta_{\mathrm{KL}}\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}\left[\pi_{\theta}\,\|\,\pi_{\mathrm{ref}}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where
<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho_{j}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{j}|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(y_{j}|x)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho_{j}(\theta)=\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{j}|x)}{\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}(y_{j}|x)},</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>old</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{\text{old}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the policy used for sampling.
While experiments use the standard GRPO training objective,
for theoretical analysis, we consider a surrogate <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\tilde{L}}_{\text{GRPO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\tilde{L}}_{\text{GRPO}}</annotation></semantics></math>
obtained by ignoring clipping and KL regularization terms and
assuming on-policy sampling, yielding</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{\tilde{L}}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\left[\hat{A}_{j}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{j}|x)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{\tilde{L}}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\left[\hat{A}_{j}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{j}|x)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Taking gradients yields the policy update</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{\tilde{L}}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}[\hat{A}_{j}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{j}|x)]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{\tilde{L}}_{\text{GRPO}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}[\hat{A}_{j}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y_{j}|x)].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.5" class="ltx_p">This objective provides a stable policy-gradient estimator
without requiring a learned value function and serves as the
shared optimization backbone for all methods compared in this paper.
While we instantiate GradAlign using GRPO, the framework only
requires on-policy gradient estimates and can be readily combined
with other on-policy RL objectives.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>GradAlign</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GradAlign</span>, an online data selection method for reinforcement learning. In realistic RL settings, training data is abundant but highly heterogeneous, and reward signals can be noisy or uninformative to learning. Accuracy-based filtering methods are commonly used in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, based on the intuition that LLMs learn most from problems that are neither too easy nor too hard and lie near the decision boundary. However, accuracy alone does not distinguish which problems induce beneficial policy updates. GradAlign addresses this limitation by selecting high-quality samples through gradient alignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our key insight is that a training problem is valuable only if updating the policy on it improves performance on a trustworthy validation set representing the downstream task. We approximate this improvement using first-order policy gradients: GradAlign treats the average policy gradient on the validation set as a target direction and ranks candidate problems by their alignment with this direction. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2.3 LLM Reinforcement Learning ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, GradAlign operates iteratively by estimating validation gradients, selecting aligned training problems, and updating the policy, while resampling rollouts in each round to adapt to the evolving policy.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Validation Improvement as a Proxy</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike LLM fine-tuning, where detailed solutions are provided in training data, in RL settings, we only observe reward signals. This makes it difficult to estimate the utility of individual training problems directly.
We therefore use <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">validation improvement</em> as a proxy for problem utility: a training problem is useful if updating the policy on it improves performance on the validation set <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is more likely to transfer to the held-out test set. A direct approach would be to perform RL updates on each candidate problem and measure the resulting change on <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, but this is computationally prohibitive.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Validation Improvement via Policy Gradients</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The accuracy change is a discrete signal, which makes a precise ranking of candidates for training expensive. We find that the gradient of the on-policy GRPO loss can serve as a surrogate for accuracy change, and, in fact, we have the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Under on-policy sampling, binary rewards, unbiased advantage estimation
without normalization, and ignoring KL regularization and clipping,
the GRPO gradient is an unbiased estimator of the gradient of expected accuracy.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}[R(x,y)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},\,y\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|x)}[R(x,y)]</annotation></semantics></math> be the expected reward, where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R(x,y)=\mathbf{1}\!\left[y=y^{*}(x)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(x,y)=\mathbf{1}\!\left[y=y^{*}(x)\right]</annotation></semantics></math> is the judge outcome indicating whether <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> matches the reference answer <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{*}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{*}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> equals the expected accuracy.
Under on-policy sampling, the policy gradient theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton et al., 1999</a>)</cite> gives</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\!\left[Q^{\pi}(x,y)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\!\left[Q^{\pi}(x,y)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Here <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{\pi}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{\pi}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the action-value function, i.e., the expected
return obtained by taking action <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> under policy <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> given input <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pi}(x,y)=Q^{\pi}(x,y)-b(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pi}(x,y)=Q^{\pi}(x,y)-b(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the advantage function,
where <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is any baseline independent of <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\theta}}[b(x)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)]=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{y\sim\pi_{\theta}}[b(x)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)]=0.</annotation></semantics></math>
Therefore</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\!\left[A^{\pi}(x,y)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\right]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\!\left[A^{\pi}(x,y)\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">If the advantage estimator <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is unbiased, i.e.,
<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\hat{A}\mid x,y]=A^{\pi}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>π</mi></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\hat{A}\mid x,y]=A^{\pi}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\!\left[\hat{A}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\right]=\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x,y\sim\pi_{\theta}}\!\left[\hat{A}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(y\mid x)\right]=\nabla_{\theta}J(\theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_text">showing that the resulting update is an unbiased estimator of the policy gradient.
In particular, GRPO forms its advantage by subtracting the expected return baseline that is
independent of the sampled action under the stated assumptions,
and therefore satisfies this condition.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We therefore approximate the expected validation accuracy change using the gradient of the GRPO objective. Based on Thm <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.2 Validation Improvement via Policy Gradients ‣ 4 GradAlign ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, under a first-order approximation, the validation improvement induced by a candidate training problem is proportional to the inner product between the policy gradients computed on the validation set and on the candidate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In real RL training practice, the advantage estimator term <math id="S4.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is normalized for stable training. As stated in the following theorem, the direction of the policy gradient is still aligned with the expected accuracy gradient:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Based on <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4.2 Validation Improvement via Policy Gradients ‣ 4 GradAlign ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, when advantage normalization is applied, the GRPO gradient becomes biased in magnitude but remains directionally aligned, in expectation, with the expected accuracy gradient.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Advantage normalization applies an affine transformation with
a positive scaling factor to the raw advantage estimator
<math id="S4.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}^{\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>raw</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}^{\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math>, producing the normalized advantage</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}=\frac{\hat{A}^{\text{raw}}-\mu}{\sigma+\epsilon}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>raw</mtext></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}=\frac{\hat{A}^{\text{raw}}-\mu}{\sigma+\epsilon},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denote the mean and standard deviation
of <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}^{\text{raw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>raw</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}^{\text{raw}}</annotation></semantics></math> computed over the sampled batch,
and <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma+\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma+\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The quantity <math id="S4.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is constant with respect to individual sampled actions.
This changes the magnitude of the gradient but preserves its direction.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Because gradients are typically normalized in practice, their direction is more important than their magnitude. Therefore, we use cosine similarity as the alignment score in GradAlign instead of the inner product. This choice is further supported by our ablation studies (Section <a href="#S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" title="Inner product vs Cosine Similarity ‣ 7 Ablation Study ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Algorithm Details</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In particular, for each problem <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the validation set <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{v}=\{p^{v}_{1},...,p^{v}_{|\mathcal{P}_{v}|}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{v}=\{p^{v}_{1},...,p^{v}_{|\mathcal{P}_{v}|}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we roll out <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{i,1},...,o_{i,k_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{i,1},...,o_{i,k_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> with rewards <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i,1},...,r_{i,k_{v}}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i,1},...,r_{i,k_{v}}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math> (correct or not) and compute the normalized advantage
<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{A}_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{A}_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math> following the GRPO surrogate objective
defined in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 GRPO as the Reinforcement Learning Objective ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Then, the gradient <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for problem <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated by:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle g^{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle g^{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{\tilde{L}}_{\text{GRPO}}(p^{v}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>GRPO</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{\tilde{L}}_{\text{GRPO}}(p^{v}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{k_{v}}\sum_{j=1}^{k_{v}}\hat{A}_{i,j}\,\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}\!\left(o_{i,j}\mid p^{v}_{i}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{k_{v}}\sum_{j=1}^{k_{v}}\hat{A}_{i,j}\,\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}\!\left(o_{i,j}\mid p^{v}_{i}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Averaging all gradients, we obtain the validation policy gradient <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{P}_{v}|}\sum_{i=1}g^{v}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{P}_{v}|}\sum_{i=1}g^{v}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In each data selection round, for each candidate training problem <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r,i}\in\mathcal{P}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r,i}\in\mathcal{P}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, similarly, we estimate the GRPO policy gradient <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{r,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{r,i}</annotation></semantics></math> using sampled rollouts <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since the training set is substantially larger than the validation set (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}_{r}|\gg|\mathcal{P}_{v}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≫</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}_{r}|\gg|\mathcal{P}_{v}|</annotation></semantics></math>), we use fewer rollouts (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{r}&lt;k_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{r}&lt;k_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>) for training candidates, as high-precision gradient estimates are less critical in this case. Finally, we rank the candidate training problems according to the cosine similarity between <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{r,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{r,i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>, selecting the top-<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S_{r}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S_{r}|</annotation></semantics></math> candidates.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that our settings and design differ from previous methods based on the influence function that requires fully trained models and second-order information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Koh and Liang, 2017</a>)</cite>. Second-order effects are unnecessary in our setting, as each candidate example is used for only a small number of updates, for which first-order approximations are sufficient.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>On-Policy Resampling</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because the policy evolves during RL training, both the expected validation accuracy and the validation GRPO gradients change over time. To mitigate the bias introduced by stale rollouts in advantage estimation, we resample all validation problems and recompute the average validation gradient at the beginning of each data-selection round.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This design differs from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhu et al. (2025)</a></cite>, which estimates gradients using offline rollouts with importance sampling. Although importance sampling can be unbiased, as policy evolves, newly emerging behaviors may not be covered by the offline rollout distribution, preventing reliable estimation of their gradients. Moreover, importance sampling typically incurs high variance when the behavior and target policies diverge, requiring substantially more samples for stable estimation and increasing computational cost.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overall Algorithm.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our algorithm is listed in Algo. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Overall Algorithm. ‣ 4.4 On-Policy Resampling ‣ 4 GradAlign ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21492v1/distribution_1.svg" id="S4.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/316;" width="476" height="316"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Unreliable reward signals</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21492v1/distribution_2.svg" id="S4.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/316;" width="476" height="316"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution imbalance</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S4.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2602.21492v1/distribution_3.svg" id="S4.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/316;" width="476" height="316"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F2.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Low-Utility Training Data</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Illustration of three challenging data-selection scenarios.<span id="S4.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Each panel shows a failure mode where accuracy-based filtering fails to
identify training samples that improve downstream performance.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Grad-Aligned Data Selection (<span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">GradAlign</span>)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> initial policy <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>; validation set <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>; candidate pools <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{P}_{r}\}_{r=1}^{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>R</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{P}_{r}\}_{r=1}^{R}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{P}_{r}|=M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{P}_{r}|=M</annotation></semantics></math>; selection ratio <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> (select <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/q</annotation></semantics></math> candidates per round); number of rollouts <math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="alg1.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="alg1.l1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> for advantage normalization.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> trained policy <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">(Resample &amp; compute validation gradient)</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
                Initialize <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each validation problem <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{v}\in\mathcal{P}_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{v}\in\mathcal{P}_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                 Roll out <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{o_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k_{v}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid p^{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{o_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k_{v}}\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot\mid p^{v})</annotation></semantics></math> and obtain rewards <math id="alg1.l7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{r_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k_{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{r_{j}\}_{j=1}^{k_{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                 Compute GRPO gradient:<math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{v}_{p}\leftarrow\frac{1}{k_{v}}\sum_{j=1}^{k_{v}}\hat{A}_{j}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(o_{j}\mid p^{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{v}_{p}\leftarrow\frac{1}{k_{v}}\sum_{j=1}^{k_{v}}\hat{A}_{j}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta}(o_{j}\mid p^{v})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
                 Accumulate <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}\leftarrow G_{v}+g^{v}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>p</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}\leftarrow G_{v}+g^{v}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
                
                
                Average <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{v}\leftarrow\frac{1}{|\mathcal{P}_{v}|}G_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{v}\leftarrow\frac{1}{|\mathcal{P}_{v}|}G_{v}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">(Score candidates by gradient alignment)</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> each candidate problem <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{r,i}\in\mathcal{P}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{r,i}\in\mathcal{P}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>



</div>
<div id="alg1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
                
                
                 Roll out <math id="alg1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> samples and compute its GRPO gradient estimate <math id="alg1.l14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{r,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{r,i}</annotation></semantics></math> (same procedure as above).

</div>
<div id="alg1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
                
                
                 Score <math id="alg1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{r,i}\leftarrow\cos(g_{r,i},G_{v})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{r,i}\leftarrow\cos(g_{r,i},G_{v})</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l16.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
                
                
                Select <math id="alg1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{r}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{r}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> top-<math id="alg1.l17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M/q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M/q</annotation></semantics></math> candidates in <math id="alg1.l17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="alg1.l17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{r,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{r,i}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l18.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">(RL update on selected curriculum)</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
                
                
                Update <math id="alg1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> by GRPO training on <math id="alg1.l19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
                
                
               <span id="alg1.l20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l20.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Considerations</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Applicable Scenarios</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GradAlign is particularly well-suited for settings where the training rewards are noisy or unreliable, as the gradients are more dispersed. We highlight three common scenarios, with illustrations in Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Overall Algorithm. ‣ 4.4 On-Policy Resampling ‣ 4 GradAlign ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Unreliable Reward Signals</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In many RL settings, the reward signal can be noisy or unreliable, providing an imperfect proxy for true task performance. For example, in LLM RL, rewards are often obtained from model-based judges, and such rewards can be inaccurate on ambiguous problems or inputs beyond the judge’s capabilities. These cases often exhibit intermediate accuracy and are therefore difficult to remove with accuracy-based filtering, which can waste compute and destabilize training. GradAlign is well-suited to this setting: gradients induced by misjudged problems tend to be weakly aligned with the validation gradient (often near-orthogonal in high-dimensional space), so GradAlign naturally downweights them. In Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Experiments ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we show that GradAlign improves the stability of the training and the final performance under unreliable reward signals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distribution Imbalance</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When the training data set contains a wide range of problems (e.g., math problems containing different categories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Albalak et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) and the target downstream task is more specific (e.g., one specific puzzle game), many training problems may be correct and of moderate difficulty, but uninformative for the target task, resulting in accuracy-based methods failing in filtering such problems. GradAlign addresses this mismatch by favoring problems whose gradients align with the validation gradient, while downweighting target-irrelevant ones with low alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Low-Utility Training Data</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As RL training scales up, practitioners increasingly rely on large web-mined datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Unlike distribution imbalance, where useful data is underrepresented, the challenge here is that many training problems are on-distribution but overly simple, yielding valid rewards while providing limited learning signal for the target task. GradAlign addresses this setting by prioritizing problems whose gradients align with validation performance and downweighting low-utility samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Computational Overhead</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GradAlign introduces additional computations for estimating gradient alignment, but the overhead is modest for three reasons.
First, the validation set is small, so computing the target gradient incurs a negligible cost relative to policy training.
Second, gradient computation for training candidates adds only a constant-factor overhead compared to accuracy-based filtering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaplan et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
Finally, by prioritizing more informative training problems, GradAlign improves sample efficiency, reducing the number of ineffective updates and leading to better final performance than training on the full dataset.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we evaluate the effectiveness of GradAlign across diverse scenarios. Through these experiments, we aim to verify the capability of GradAlign to select high-quality training data targeting specific domains from a large and noisy training dataset.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RL Framework</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All experiments are conducted using GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We use a sparse binary reward that indicates whether the final answer is correct, and all rewards are provided using Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct as a judge. Detailed RL training hyperparameters can be found in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Detailed Hyper-parameters ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scenario 1: Distinguishability Test with Noisy Rewards</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To rigorously assess the selector’s capacity to discriminate valid learning signals from stochastic noise, we establish a controlled experiment involving systematic perturbation of the training set. Utilizing the DAPO training corpus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we introduce controlled corruptions to 50% of the samples, whereby rewards are decoupled from model outputs and instead sampled from a Bernoulli distribution (<math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.5</annotation></semantics></math>).
This procedure generates a subset of data that statistically mimics the difficulty of “complex” problems (50% pass rate) while devoid of helpful gradient information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scenario 2: Unbalanced Dataset</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate alignment capabilities under distributional shift, we construct a heterogeneous dataset comprising 4k Countdown Game problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gandhi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, 20k WebInstruct problems, and 10k DAPO problems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Given that the target domain (Countdown Game) constitutes merely <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>10% of the aggregate pool, this setting challenges methods to retrieve domain-specific knowledge from imbalanced distributions. We employ a selection ratio of <math id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q=20</annotation></semantics></math> (selecting the top 5%), with validation and test sets consisting exclusively of Countdown Game problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scenario 3: Low-Utility Training Data</h5>

<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this setting, we utilize the WebInstruct-filtered-unverified dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>)</cite> as the training set, which is a large-scale collection (808k problems) gathered from internet sources that represents typical noisy, uncurated training data. The downstream tasks and validation/test splits are specified as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMLU-Pro:</span>
We train Qwen2.5-1.5B-Math-Instruct on WebInstruct. The target domain is the Math category of MMLU-Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which is partitioned into validation and test sets. This setting evaluates GradAlign’s ability to identify training problems that induce policy updates beneficial to multiple-choice mathematical reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SuperGPQA &amp; TheoremQA:</span>
We investigate scenarios where the training distribution is broad but the target domain is narrow. Specifically, we train Qwen3-8B-Base on <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">WebInstruct</span>. To improve computational efficiency, we apply a preliminary difficulty filter (pass rates <math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\in[0.2,0.8]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.8</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\in[0.2,0.8]</annotation></semantics></math>) before RL selection. We utilize SuperGPQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and TheoremQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> as the downstream target domains.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mathematical Reasoning Task:</span>
We train Qwen3-8B-Base on the same pre-filtered WebInstruct subset described above. Data selection is guided by AMC22 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mathematical Association of America, 2022</a>)</cite> as the validation set, while performance is evaluated on AMC23 and AIME24/25. This setting tests whether GradAlign can leverage a small, competition-level validation set to extract similarly challenging reasoning problems from a broad and noisy training distribution.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare GradAlign with three data selection strategies:</p>
<ol id="S6.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Random Selection:</span> Uniformly samples training data. Serves as a lower bound.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy Greedy (AccGreedy):</span> Prioritizes samples with pass rates closest to 50%, based on the heuristic that problems near the decision boundary provide the strongest signal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LearnAlign (Align)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>: A method that selects data based on gradient similarity within the training set without using external validation guidance.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Experimental Results</h3>

