<span id="S6.T2.8.1.1.1.1.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.45pt 2.0pt;"><span id="S6.T2.8.1.1.1.1.1.5.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">47.5%</span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span><span id="S6.T2.8.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S6.T2.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ratio of corrupted problems in selected data (Scenario 1).</span> GradAlign selects the lowest ratio of corrupted data in the selected dataset.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.3.1 Results on Noisy Rewards ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the results of the controlled noise injection experiment. GradAlign significantly outperforms all baselines on most datasets, except for AMC23 at step 50. AccGreedy fails because it selects corrupted data (which have pass rates near 50%) as high-signal samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also report the ratio of corrupted problems selected at steps 0, 50, and 100 in Table <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.3.1 Results on Noisy Rewards ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. GradAlign consistently selects the lowest proportion of corrupted data. AccGreedy selects the most corrupted data (over 80%) because the corrupted samples have pass rates near 50%. Figure <a href="#A1.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Appendix A Detailed Hyper-parameters ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the training accuracy curves, where GradAlign consistently outperforms all baselines.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.3.2 </span>Results on Unbalanced Dataset</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.3.2 Results on Unbalanced Dataset ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows results for the unbalanced dataset setting. GradAlign achieves the best performance on both validation and test sets, followed by Align. Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6.3.2 Results on Unbalanced Dataset ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the ratio of Countdown Game problems selected. AccGreedy rarely selects these problems because of the low initial accuracy on Countdown Game data. This result demonstrates the importance of gradient-informed data selection for domain-specific filtering.</p>
</div>
