<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span><span id="S6.T5.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance on SuperGPQA, TheoremQA, mathematical reasoning, and MMLU-Pro (Scenario 3).</span> Best results are in <span id="S6.T5.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span>; second-best are <span id="S6.T5.13" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">underlined</span>. The training set is WebInstruct, and the base model is Qwen3-8B-Base (except MMLU-Pro, where the base model is Qwen2.5-1.5B-Math-Instruct).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.3.3 </span>Performance on Low-Utility Data</h4>

<div id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate performance using WebInstruct as the training set, a large-scale automatically constructed dataset. GradAlign achieves the highest average performance across all settings, while some baseline methods remain competitive on specific splits.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SuperGPQA and TheoremQA:</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6.3.2 Results on Unbalanced Dataset ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, GradAlign performs best at step 100, achieving 59.3% on TheoremQA-test compared to 54.9% for AccGreedy and 57.9% for Align. AccGreedy achieves 60.4% at step 50 but degrades to 54.9% at step 100. This suggests AccGreedy quickly exploits the strongest learning signals but subsequently suffers from noise and limited diversity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mathematical Reasoning:</span> GradAlign achieves the best results on most datasets (Table <a href="#S6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 6.3.2 Results on Unbalanced Dataset ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). While AccGreedy performs well on AIME24-25 at step 50, GradAlign maintains higher consistency across steps, achieving the best average pass rate (42.8%). In the MMLU-Pro setting, GradAlign also outperforms the baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We also note that although GradAlign requires more computation per step, it converges faster and achieves higher final performance than random selection. Table <a href="#S6.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 6.3.3 Performance on Low-Utility Data ‣ 6.3 Experimental Results ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> compares the best performance within the first 100 steps of GradAlign with the first 250 steps of random selection. After step 120, random selection shows decreasing performance, indicating that noise in the training data limits further improvement with additional training iterations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T6" class="ltx_table">
<p id="S6.T6.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S6.T6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:232.0pt;height:61.7pt;vertical-align:-28.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">Dataset</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">GradAlign (Ours)</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">Random</span></span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;"></span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">(Best Acc.@Step)</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">(Best Acc.@Step)</span></span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">AMC22-val</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">50.1</span>@95</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">48.5@75</span></span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">AIME2425-test</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">20.3</span>@55</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">18.5@105</span></span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">AMC23-test</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;"><span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">65.1</span>@90</span>
<span id="S6.T6.3.1.1.1.1.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding:0.45pt 4.0pt;">61.0@120</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span><span id="S6.T6.3.2" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span><span id="S6.T6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Best Performance During Training (Scenario 3).</span>
We report the best accuracy achieved during training, along with the corresponding training step.
Although Random is trained with a larger budget (250 steps vs. 100 steps for GradAlign), its performance saturates early and remains consistently worse than GradAlign.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Ablation Study</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct ablation studies on three design choices: the alignment score metric (cosine similarity versus inner product), the sample size required for stable gradient estimation, and the performance of training directly on the validation set instead of the training set.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inner product vs Cosine Similarity</h5>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare cosine similarity and inner product as alignment score measures in the noisy reward setting. As shown in Table <a href="#S7.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Inner product vs Cosine Similarity ‣ 7 Ablation Study ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, cosine similarity achieves higher performance on most datasets. Figure <a href="#A1.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix A Detailed Hyper-parameters ‣ GradAlign: Gradient-Aligned Data Selection forLLM Reinforcement Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that cosine similarity better separates clean samples from corrupted samples.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T7" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S7.T7.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:155.3pt;height:64.3pt;vertical-align:-30.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-55.2pt,22.9pt) scale(0.58443499897926,0.58443499897926) ;">
